  "finish": {
    "type": "finish",
    "reason": "candidate_ready",
    "explanation": "Candidate ff00b9e9 (patch 7098ddcd) edits only sqlalchemy_mixins/eagerload.py and sqlalchemy_mixins/smartquery.py. SQLAlchemy 2.0 removed string relationship names in loader options, so _resolve_attrs walks a dotted path to class-bound mapped attributes, _eager_option chains joinedload/subqueryload per level, _eager_expr_from_flat_schema emits one maximal chain per schema path (avoiding the loader-strategy conflict that a parent subqueryload plus separate top-level child joinedload produced), and _contains_eager_option builds an of_type() alias chain over the aliases created for relation filters and sorts. Standalone smart_query converts an AppenderQuery (lazy='dynamic') to a plain Query when the removed legacy state is unavailable. All six visible public checks pass on 2.0.38 on this revision (observation:34d71d8f), while the unmodified source fails three of the same checks on 2.0.38 and passes them on 1.4.54, so the string-loader-path failure is a real version regression, not a test artifact. The documented nested schema form {'posts': (SUBQUERY, {'comments': JOINED})} was measured on this revision against the plain {'posts': SUBQUERY} form: both emit 2 statements, delta 0 (observation:75375508). That oracle is a same-environment delta, so it does not establish an absolute query count. Standalone smart_query on a bare lazy='dynamic' relationship query returns the same row count as the equivalent plain Query, delta 0 (observation:fff4c301). Known limitation for review: applying smart_query to a dynamic query that already carries limit/offset raises InvalidRequestError ('Query.filter() being called on a Query which already has LIMIT or OFFSET applied'); the unmodified 1.4.54 source raises the same error class, so this is a pre-existing library limitation rather than a candidate-introduced regression, and the earlier limit probe is inconclusive as a numeric oracle. Remaining unobserved for independent final acceptance: inspection.attribute-names (columns, primary_keys, relations, settable_relations without viewonly, hybrid_properties, hybrid_methods, settable_attributes; inspection.py is unchanged and exercised only indirectly through fill()) and the full cross-version migration statement. No counterexamples are open. No files were added, removed or renamed; no tests or dependencies were edited; the library still only flushes and never commits on the caller's behalf. Submitting for review is not a claim of final acceptance.",
